            "creates the directory with the exact permission bits `permissions` (`mkdir(trimmed_path, permissions)`, then `fchmod(2)` on a no-follow descriptor); the transient mode is `permissions & ~umask` plus the kernel-inherited `S_ISGID` of a setgid parent, which the final `fchmod` clears, never more permissive in the permission bits the caller controls, and a umask that removes the owner read bit fails closed with `permission_denied`; a default ACL on the parent can grant access during the creation window until the final `fchmod` updates the ACL mask, and the guarantee covers the mode bits this call controls; trailing slashes are trimmed before every no-follow operation, so `\"d/\"` cannot turn a final symbolic link into a followed prefix component; `permissions` MUST be in `0..0o777`, otherwise `invalid_argument` before any system call; the final mode is applied on the descriptor, never by path, so a path swapped for a symbolic link cannot redirect it (`invalid_path`); the descriptor is compared with the device and inode captured after `mkdir`, and a different directory is `invalid_path` without any `fchmod`; a directory swapped between `mkdir` and the status capture becomes the expected status, since the parent directory is the trust boundary; the descriptor step needs the owner read bit to survive the umask, so a request without owner read (for example `0o300`, `0o200`, `0o100`, `0o000`) or a umask that strips it (for example `0o750` under `umask 0o700`) fails closed with `permission_denied` for a process subject to DAC (root bypasses the check) and removes the directory it created; the final mode is exact for every mode and umask that leave the owner able to open the transient directory; cleanup compares the device and inode immediately before the `rmdir`, but the check and the removal are separate syscalls, so the guarantee is best-effort and a swap in the remaining window is not detectable; if that guarded removal fails, the original error names the remaining path; a failure of the no-follow `open`, the descriptor `statx`/identity check or the `fchmod` runs the guarded cleanup, while a post-creation path `statx` failure returns that error with `directory_path` as subject and may leave the created directory (the descriptor `close` result is not checked); the directory already exists (or a file or a symbolic link of the same name) → `already_exists`; missing parent → `not_found`; a parent component that is not a directory → `not_a_directory`; empty path → `not_found`."),
