            "**depth-first pre-order** traversal; siblings sorted by name (UTF-16 ordinal); root **excluded**; files, directories and links all listed; links to directories listed but **not traversed**; each entry's `path` equals `root_path / relative_path`; the names are read as raw bytes by `opendir`/`readdir`/`closedir` (P/Invoke,), which allows detecting a name that is not valid UTF-8; non-directory root → `not_a_directory`; if `root_path` is a symbolic link to a directory, it is **followed**; manual recursion (no stack overflow). Each `directory_entry` carries its `kind` (`\"file\"`,`\"directory\"`,`\"symlink\"`,`\"other\"`) obtained **without extra metadata**: the `d_type` returned by `readdir` answers directly for any known type, only `DT_UNKNOWN` (some file systems) falls back to a `statx` **without following links**. It **does not abort on a per-entry error**. It returns a `walk_report`: `entries` holds, in traversal order, every `directory_entry` that could be read; `skipped_paths` holds, in the same traversal order, one `walk_skip` per path that could not be listed or decoded. A directory that cannot be listed keeps its own entry in `entries` (when its parent listed it), its children are omitted, and one `walk_skip` records that directory path with `kind` `permission_denied` (or `other` for another listing failure). A child name that is not valid UTF-8 is omitted from `entries` and recorded as `invalid_encoding`; because such a name has no valid text, the `path_text` field is its lenient UTF-8 decoding (U+FFFD for each invalid byte or subpart) so the skip still names the child. A failure to read `root_path` itself (not found, permission denied, not a directory, invalid path) is still an `error`, never a partial report; an undecodable name below the root is a skip, not an error. A caller that needs the old strict behavior (fail the whole walk if anything was skipped) tests `report.skipped_paths.length() > 0`. > Note (known limitation): the `kind` gain exists only on a file system that populates `d_type` (`ext4`, `tmpfs`, `xfs`, `btrfs`…). On a system that systematically returns `DT_UNKNOWN`, every entry pays the `statx` fallback (and an entry may be `statx`ed twice: once for its nature, once to decide the descent); the traversal remains **correct** but the performance advantage disappears."),
